@marine_vet  @marine_vet Charlie Kirk isn’t wrong that many women today feel “lost,” but the cause isn’t simply choosing career over family. What’s missing in his argument is that society pressures women to do everything—be high-achieving at work, be perfect mothers, stay attractive, run a household—without the structural support (affordable childcare, stable wages, work-life balance) to make that possible.

That’s why so many women report less happiness today than past generations: not because family isn’t valued, but because career demands and cultural expectations have crowded it out. The truth is family provides the deepest, most lasting source of meaning and stability, and when society pushes women to put career above that, it creates burnout and dissatisfaction.

If we want women to thrive, the solution isn’t to erase career goals—it’s to reset priorities, make family the foundation, and build careers around that, not the other way around.

